DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The xxoo__ppaarrssee__aarrggss() function is used to process command-line arguments,
     which are described in xo_options(7).  lliibbxxoo specific options are pro‐
     cessed and removed from the argument list so the calling application does
     not need to process them.  If successful, a new value for argc is re‐
     turned.  On failure, a message it emitted and -1 is returned.

               argc = xo_parse_args(argc, argv);
               if (argc < 0)
                   exit(EXIT_FAILURE);

     Following the call to xxoo__ppaarrssee__aarrggss(), the application can process the
     remaining arguments in a normal manner.

     The xxoo__sseett__pprrooggrraamm() function sets name of the program as reported by
     functions like xxoo__ffaaiilluurree(), xxoo__wwaarrnn(), xxoo__eerrrr(), etc.  The program name
     is initialized by xxoo__ppaarrssee__aarrggss(), but subsequent calls to
     xxoo__sseett__pprrooggrraamm() can override this value.

     Note that the value is not copied, so the memory passed to
     xxoo__sseett__pprrooggrraamm() (and xxoo__ppaarrssee__aarrggss()) must be maintained by the caller.

